// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [ssTEA Configuration Basics.note]    search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The default configuration of simpleSAL/ssTEA "out of the box" is that nothing is enabled.

     The configuration file contains the specification of all configurable options of simpleSAL,
     with every one of them commented out and each one of them paired with an explanatory comment.
     This allows for several alternatives in specifying the configuration of a project or projects.

     Major choice: do not include "ss_configuration.h"
       As described below, the IDE's inclusion policies block a user from this configuration style.
       The configuration file establishes this mesa/App's solution to ssTEA's mesa dependencies.

     Major choice: include "ss_configuration.h"
     Minor choice:
     1) leave ssTEA not configured and not enabled, "unedited" content
     2) configure ssTEA
        a) use the simplest configuration, as enabled and defined in the unedited configuration file
        b) use a complex configuration, defined in the edited configuration file for this project
        c) use a complex configuration, defined in the User source file; unedited configuration file
        d) use a complex configuration, defined by run-time build parameters specific to a project;
                    source files and configuration files do not need editing to build a version.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   At some level, an Arduino build in the IDE makes restrictions or assumptions to support "easy".
   At its most basic: included file contents require a local subdirectory prefix, unfortunately
   Arduino IDE 2.4.1.1 will find every file in the directory tree and include it, whether named in
   a source file or not.  This is very helpful but a professional programmer would not depend on it.
   In addition, sophisticated source code analysis tools are very helpful and would not allow it.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   for _ONEOF_ options: one must be uncommented, defined; all others must be commented, not defined.
     All _ONEOF_ options are prefixed, all options with the same prefix are mutually exclusive.
   for _OPTIN_ options: uncommenting the #define will choose to build in a functionality or feature.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Most #defines control individual subcomponents, after a component was included at the top level.
   The toppiest of the top levels is ssTEA itself; this is why inclusion of ssTEA is a User choice.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #ifdef SS_OPTIN_INCLUDE_SSTEA
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   All data allocations concerning events depend on or are based on the number of events requested:
   The underlying AboutEv database, the ssUI API event database, and the ssTEA Event Info database.
   An App may override the simplest or complex configurations by defining a count before including.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Launching an Event requires an AboutEv for the Launcher. Pending: a stack-based AboutEv Launcher.
   This is not a design issue, this is getting an implementation completed with ssTEA_App examples...
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #ifndef SSE_ACTIVE_EVENTS_CT_REQUESTED
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The complexity? 1) a change to any #define here will be invisible without rejecting simplest,
   2) rejecting the simplest configuration results in the activation of, and effect of, the
   set of options that are complex and powerful, but more important, coherent with each other.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #ifndef SS_ACCEPT_SIMPLEST_CONFIG
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A "UI" is a presentation layer for the user of an App.  The data types provided by the ssHL
// compiler are supported only by the processor's data type building block: integers.  Values that
// exist in memory must be brought into processor reqisters to be compared, changed, or moved.
// Collections of processor data types are supported by processor addressing mechanisms: array[i].
// An "Ascii character" is simply the assignment of a UI value ('A') to a binary value with 8 bits.
// The uiOps are not concerned with the value of characters in an array except for the value zero.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ssTEA has an optional OPTIN User Interface, where the User is the software developer using ssTEA
// to implement an App.  The UI is output-only, emitting Ascii characters representing states within
// ssTEA or integer values in decimal or hexadecimal form.  ssUI displays information for the public
// ssTEA environment at all levels; ssUI is as ignorant of the inner workings of ssTEA as the App.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   configurable ssTEA UI requirements

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The definition of the way in which Time Works is either "in a line from A to B" or "as an FSM".
//   "in a line" means: only addition, no subtraction, no support for negative Time values.
//   "FSM" means: the same ability to do math operations as with integers: addition and subtraction.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One reason to have a variety is they support ssTEA experiments with computational load tradeoffs.
// There are multiple ways of deciding if TimeA is before TimeB.  If option A has a useful feature
// but it takes more time and energy to compare Time values, how valuable is that useful feature?
// If option B is much faster and uses less energy, what features does option B give up for that?
// If option B brings extra software (in memory) that your code actually never calls, is that OK?
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #define      SST_ONEOF_TIME_IS_AN_ARROW
   #define      SST_ONEOF_TIME_IS_AN_FSM

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
